select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b04-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"In an electricity bill, which charge answers each question: how fast, how much and when is electricity used?","back":"How fast: maximum demand charge (kVA). How much: energy charge (kWh). When: time-of-day (TOD) peak/off-peak charges.","explain":"The power factor charge reflects real power versus apparent power. Other factors in the final price include HT/LT tariffs, slab rates, consumer category (industrial, commercial, agricultural etc.), regional tariffs, incentives/penalties and tax holidays for new projects.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p122","order":1209},
{"id":"p1-b04-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What two features should an energy benchmarking tool have?","back":"1) A common metric irrespective of plant size, most commonly energy intensity (energy use per unit of output). 2) It should be used across a wide range of facilities, to compensate for production differences at similar facilities.","explain":"Energy benchmarking compares the performance of a plant or sector against a common metric that represents 'standard' or 'optimal' performance. It forms the basis for monitoring and target setting.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p122","order":1210},
{"id":"p1-b04-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three approaches to industrial energy benchmarking?","back":"1) Evaluating an entire industrial sector (vs best available technology, other countries, or over time). 2) Comparing individual plants within a sector. 3) A company using its own historical best performance as the benchmark (internal).","explain":"In the second approach, proprietary data is an issue, so indicators must be general enough to reveal nothing confidential. The third approach avoids this because benchmarking is done internally.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p122","order":1211},
{"id":"p1-b04-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which factors must be checked for similarity before external (inter-unit) benchmarking? Is energy price one of them?","back":"Scale of operation, vintage of technology, raw material specifications and quality, and product specifications and quality. Energy price is NOT one of them.","explain":"External benchmarking compares a group of similar units to identify best practices. If these factors differ, the findings can be grossly misleading. This exact trap appears as an objective question in the book.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p123","order":1212},
{"id":"p1-b04-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"List the steps in energy conservation benchmarking.","back":"1) Identify the best available technology for each process unit. 2) Understand the process and identify key controlling parameters. 3) Determine the unit's performance. 4) Analyse the gap against the benchmark. 5) Set targets within constraints and implement improvements.","explain":"Gap analysis on the key controlling parameters is what turns a benchmark into an action list. Targets must keep practical constraints in view.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p123","order":1213},
{"id":"p1-b04-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Give the BEE benchmark parameters for: cement plant, textile unit, power plant, A/C plant, compressed air and DG power plant.","back":"Cement: kWh/MT clinker or cement. Textile: kWh/kg yarn. Power plant: kcal/kWh (heat rate). A/C: kWh per ton of refrigeration. Compressed air: kWh/Nm³. DG plant: kWh per litre.","explain":"The book splits these into gross production-related parameters (cement, textile, paper, power, fertiliser in Million kcal/MT urea or ammonia, foundry kWh/MT liquid metal) and equipment/utility-related ones (kW/TR, boiler % thermal efficiency, cooling tower % effectiveness, kWh/Nm³, kWh/litre).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p123","order":1214},
{"id":"p1-b04-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which accompanying parameters must be stated when comparing (a) a cement plant's kWh/MT, (b) a boiler's thermal efficiency, (c) an A/C plant's kW/TR?","back":"(a) Type of cement, Blaine number (fineness) and process (wet/dry). (b) Fuel quality and type, steam pressure, temperature and flow, and whether efficiency is on a GCV or NCV basis and by the direct or indirect method. (c) Parity of chilled water temperature.","explain":"Without these, comparisons among similar industries are not meaningful. Other examples: a power plant's heat rate needs % loading, condenser vacuum and inlet cooling-water temperature. Compressed air must be compared at similar inlet air temperature and generation pressure.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p124","order":1215},
{"id":"p1-b04-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Define production factor and reference year equivalent energy use.","back":"Production factor = current year's production ÷ reference year's production. Reference year equivalent = reference year energy use × production factor.","explain":"The reference year equivalent is the energy the plant would have used to make this year's output if it had operated as it did in the reference year. Comparing it with actual energy use separates efficiency changes from changes in output.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p125","order":1216},
{"id":"p1-b04-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Why do equipment capacity and user requirement often mismatch, and give examples of matching energy use to requirement.","back":"Designers add safety margins, so equipment is oversized and runs at part load. Examples: trimming impellers, VSDs or resizing to eliminate pump throttling and fan dampers; moderating chilled water temperature to process needs; back-pressure turbines instead of control-valve pressure drops; task lighting instead of area lighting.","explain":"Matching comes first in the book's sequence. It is followed by maximising system efficiencies (O&M and best technology), then optimising input energy (heat recovery, cogeneration, renewables), and then fuel/energy substitution.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p126","order":1217},
{"id":"p1-b04-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish fuel substitution from energy substitution, with examples.","back":"Fuel substitution replaces a fossil fuel with a more efficient, cheaper or cleaner fuel (e.g. natural gas in fertiliser/power; coconut shells or rice husk for coal; LSHS for LDO). Energy substitution changes the form of energy (e.g. steam heaters for electric heaters; solar for steam-based hot water).","explain":"The book names two ways to reduce energy dependency: energy conservation and substitution. Natural gas is increasingly the fuel and feedstock of choice in the fertiliser, petrochemical, power and sponge iron industries.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p127","order":1218},
{"id":"p1-b04-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"problem","front":"A textile plant replaces a furnace-oil thermic fluid heater (GCV 10,200 kcal/kg, 82% efficiency, fuel cost Rs 1,800/h) with a coconut-chip fired unit (GCV 4,200 kcal/kg, 72% efficiency, Rs 700/h) for the same 15 lakh kcal/h duty. It runs 25 days × 12 months × 24 h. Extra auxiliary power and manpower cost Rs 10 lakh/yr and the new heater costs Rs 35 lakh. Find the net saving and payback.","back":"Net saving Rs 70 lakh/yr; payback 6 months.","explain":"This is the book's fuel substitution case study. The agro-fuel has less than half the calorific value and lower efficiency, but its far lower cost per hour still gives a very short payback. The plant already handled coconut chips in another boiler, which made the change practical.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p128","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the annual operating hours.","answer":"25 × 12 × 24 = 7,200 h/yr","explain":"Days per month × months × hours per day."},{"prompt":"Find the annual furnace oil cost.","answer":"7,200 × Rs 1,800 ≈ Rs 130 lakh","explain":"Hourly fuel cost × hours (exactly Rs 129.6 lakh)."},{"prompt":"Find the annual coconut chip cost.","answer":"7,200 × Rs 700 ≈ Rs 50 lakh","explain":"Same duty and hours, at the agro-fuel's hourly cost."},{"prompt":"Find the net annual saving.","answer":"130 − 50 = Rs 80 lakh; 80 − 10 = Rs 70 lakh","explain":"Deduct the extra auxiliary power and manpower cost from the gross fuel saving."},{"prompt":"Find the simple payback.","answer":"35 ÷ 70 = 0.5 yr = 6 months","explain":"Payback = investment ÷ net annual saving."}],"order":1219},
{"id":"p1-b04-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"On what method does the Fyrite work, and which reagents absorb CO₂ and O₂?","back":"The Orsat method of volumetric analysis. Potassium hydroxide (dyed red) absorbs CO₂, and chromous chloride (blue) absorbs O₂.","explain":"A hand bellows pump draws flue gas into the solution, and the change in liquid volume shows the gas percentage. The absorbing fluid is also the indicating fluid, so one vessel does the work of both the measuring burette and the absorption pipette.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p129","order":1220},
{"id":"p1-b04-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How do a fuel efficiency monitor and a combustion gas analyser differ?","back":"The fuel efficiency monitor measures O₂ and flue gas temperature and uses stored fuel calorific values to calculate combustion efficiency. The combustion gas analyser uses replaceable chemical cells to measure gases such as CO₂, CO, NOx and SOx.","explain":"Because a gas analyser can hold a maximum of two sensors, only two or three parameters can be measured at once. It is lighter and easier to handle than a fuel efficiency monitor. The book names the combustion analyser as the instrument for O₂ and CO in flue gas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p130","order":1221},
{"id":"p1-b04-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How is air or flue gas pressure measured in a duct?","back":"A digital flexible-membrane manometer combined with a Pitot tube, inserted through a 6 cm monitoring hole in the duct.","explain":"Flexible rubber tubes connect the manometer's inlet and outlet probes to the two ends of the Pitot tube, and must be tight to avoid air leakage. This is used for exhaust flue gases from boilers and furnaces and for air from fans and blowers.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p130","order":1222},
{"id":"p1-b04-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How does a non-contact infrared thermometer determine temperature, and what must be known about the object?","back":"It measures the infrared radiation emitted by the object; knowing the object's emissivity, it calculates the temperature. A lens focuses IR onto a detector that gives an electrical signal, corrected for ambient temperature.","explain":"It suits objects in hazardous or hard-to-reach places. By contrast, a contact thermometer is a thermocouple probe inserted into flue gas, hot air or hot water, with a leaf-type probe for surface temperatures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p131","order":1223},
{"id":"p1-b04-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish transit-time and Doppler ultrasonic flow meters.","back":"Transit-time meters send signals with and against the flow; the time difference is proportional to flow rate. They are used for clean liquids. Doppler meters use the frequency shift of signals reflected off particles. They are used for dirty liquids.","explain":"Both are non-contact flow meters. An exam MCQ asks which instrument uses the transit-time method: the ultrasonic flow meter.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p131","order":1224},
{"id":"p1-b04-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which instrument gives non-contact speed measurement, and on what principle?","back":"The stroboscope. High-intensity flashes at a precise frequency make a rotating object appear slowed or stopped when the flash frequency bears a definite relationship to its speed, giving the RPM.","explain":"A tachometer is the simple contact-type alternative, used where direct access is possible. Speed matters in audits because it changes with frequency, belt slip and loading.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p132","order":1225},
{"id":"p1-b04-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What do smart energy meters and thermography each provide?","back":"Smart meters give real-time detailed usage statistics through wireless communication, viewable or controllable by mobile or internet. Thermography (IR imaging) shows hot and cold spots on surfaces for assessing equipment health and predictive maintenance.","explain":"The thermal camera converts IR radiation into electronic video signals that are amplified and shown on a monitor for interpretation. Smart meters can also be used for fuel or water.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p133","order":1226},
{"id":"p1-b04-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Under the BEE (Manner and Intervals of Time for Conduct of Energy Audit) Regulations 2008, when must a Designated Consumer's first and subsequent energy audits be done?","back":"First audit by an accredited energy auditor within 18 months of the Central Government notification. Subsequent audits every three years, counted from the date the previous audit report was submitted to the DC's management.","explain":"A common trap is counting the three years from the audit date or the notification date. The interval runs from submission of the previous report by the accredited energy auditor to the DC's management.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p134","order":1227},
{"id":"p1-b04-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Under the 2008 audit regulations, what must each list of recommendations include?","back":"A description of each measure; its technical or economic life; known technical risks; a preliminary assessment of financial attractiveness; a tabulated summary by implementation schedule (short, medium, long term); the techno-financially better option where alternatives exist; and impacts on O&M, staff and budget with solutions.","explain":"The action plan prepared jointly with the energy manager must include detailed techno-economic analysis, a monitoring and verification protocol for annual reporting to BEE and the SDA, and an agreed time schedule considering finance and equipment availability.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p135","order":1228},
{"id":"p1-b04-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Under the 2008 audit regulations, which forms does the accredited energy auditor submit to the Designated Consumer's management?","back":"Form 2: the energy audit report. Form 3: an evaluation of the implementation of each measure recommended in the previous audit report.","explain":"Both go to the management of the designated consumer. Form 3 makes each audit check whether earlier recommendations were actually carried out.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p136","order":1229},
{"id":"p1-b04-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What must the accredited energy auditor certify at the end of the energy audit report?","back":"Data collection was diligent and truthful; all monitoring devices are in good condition, calibrated or certified, and untampered; reasonable professional skill and care were taken; adequate training was given to operating personnel after implementation; and the audit followed the 2008 Regulations.","explain":"The auditor signs the report under the firm's seal with accreditation and manpower details. The report must include a work schedule sheet signed by both the accredited energy auditor and the DC's energy manager. The report structure is decided jointly by the auditor and the DC.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p136","order":1230},
{"id":"p1-b04-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"problem","front":"Reference year (2009) energy use was 12 million kcal. The production factor for 2010 is 0.9 and 2010 energy use was 11 million kcal. Find the Plant Energy Performance for 2010 and state your inference.","back":"PEP = −1.85%. Performance has marginally worsened, and management must act to improve it.","explain":"This is the book's solved example. Even though absolute energy use fell from 12 to 11 million kcal, production fell more (factor 0.9). So the energy per unit of output rose, and PEP is negative.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p137","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the reference year equivalent energy.","answer":"12 × 0.9 = 10.8 million kcal","explain":"Reference year energy × production factor is the energy the old practice would need for this year's output."},{"prompt":"Write the PEP formula.","answer":"PEP = (reference year equivalent − current year energy) ÷ reference year equivalent × 100","explain":"Positive means improvement; negative means deterioration."},{"prompt":"Calculate PEP.","answer":"(10.8 − 11) ÷ 10.8 × 100 = −1.85%","explain":"The plant used 0.2 million kcal more than the reference-year practice would have."},{"prompt":"State the inference.","answer":"Marginally negative: the energy or plant manager must act to improve performance","explain":"A falling energy bill alone does not prove better efficiency when production also falls."}],"order":1231},
{"id":"p1-052","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What principle underlies material and energy balances?","back":"Conservation of mass and energy: Input = Output + Accumulation (stored) + Losses. At steady state with nothing stored, Input = Output.","explain":"Example: 1,000 kg/h raw material in, with 700 kg product, 200 kg waste and 50 kg stored out → unaccounted loss = 1,000 − 950 = 50 kg/h. Draw a boundary around the process, list every stream crossing it, and balance one component (e.g. solids or fat) at a time.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q4, Q5","order":1232},
{"id":"p1-053","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"In concentration and drying problems, what quantity is the 'tie element' for the material balance?","back":"The non-volatile component (dissolved solids, bone-dry solids or fat), whose mass is the same in feed and product. Example: 100 kg of feed at 6% solids → 6 kg solids → product at 30% = 20 kg, so water evaporated = 80 kg.","explain":"Balance the conserved component first to get the product mass, then find the water removed by difference. Moisture on a wet basis = water ÷ (water + solids). The same logic gives salt concentration: 25 kg salt in 100 kg water = 25/125 = 20% by weight.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q11; BEE 2024 P1 Q37","order":1233},
{"id":"p1-054","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"In an energy balance, how is equipment efficiency calculated by the direct method?","back":"Efficiency = useful energy output ÷ energy input × 100. Example: boiler with 100 MJ fuel input and 70 MJ in steam → 70%. The losses (flue gas 20 MJ + radiation/unaccounted 10 MJ) make up the other 30 MJ.","explain":"In the indirect (loss) method, efficiency = 100 − Σ losses %. A complete energy balance must close: input = useful output + all losses. A large unaccounted figure points to measurement errors or a missed stream.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q5","order":1234},
{"id":"p1-055","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"A dryer receives 2,000 kg/h of wet material at 40% moisture (wet basis) and dries it to 5% moisture (wet basis). Latent heat of evaporation is 540 kcal/kg. Ignore the sensible heat of the solids. Find (a) the product rate, (b) the water evaporated, (c) the heat needed for evaporation, and (d) the steam needed if steam gives 520 kcal/kg and the dryer thermal efficiency is 60%.","back":"(a) 1,263.2 kg/h; (b) 736.8 kg/h; (c) ≈ 3.98 lakh kcal/h; (d) ≈ 1,275 kg/h of steam.","explain":"Bone-dry solids pass through unchanged, so they tie the feed and product together. The water removed is the difference between the feed and product masses. Heat for evaporation is then divided by the useful heat delivered per kg of steam.","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the bone-dry solids.","answer":"2,000 × (1 − 0.40) = 1,200 kg/h","explain":"Solids fraction = 1 − moisture (wet basis)."},{"prompt":"Find the product mass at 5% moisture.","answer":"Product = 1,200 ÷ 0.95 = 1,263.2 kg/h","explain":"Solids are 95% of the product."},{"prompt":"Find the water evaporated.","answer":"2,000 − 1,263.2 = 736.8 kg/h","explain":"Mass balance: feed = product + vapour."},{"prompt":"Find the heat for evaporation.","answer":"736.8 × 540 = 3,97,895 kcal/h","explain":"Q = m × latent heat."},{"prompt":"Find the steam required.","answer":"3,97,895 ÷ (0.60 × 520) = 1,275 kg/h","explain":"Steam = heat needed ÷ (efficiency × heat per kg of steam)."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 S7 (modified)","order":1235},
{"id":"p1-056","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"problem","front":"An evaporator is fed 4,000 kg/h of solution containing 2% solids, which is concentrated to 8% solids. Enthalpies: feed 40 kcal/kg, concentrated product 100 kcal/kg, vapour 640 kcal/kg. The heating steam gives up 540 kcal/kg (its condensate leaves at saturation). Find the vapour formed and the steam required per hour, and the steam economy.","back":"Vapour = 3,000 kg/h; steam ≈ 3,444 kg/h; steam economy ≈ 0.87 kg vapour per kg steam.","explain":"The solids balance gives the product and vapour flows. The heat balance (heat from steam + heat in feed = heat in vapour + heat in product) gives the steam needed. A single-effect evaporator has a steam economy below 1; multiple effects raise it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Solids balance to find the product.","answer":"Solids = 4,000 × 0.02 = 80 kg/h; product = 80 ÷ 0.08 = 1,000 kg/h","explain":"Solids in = solids out."},{"prompt":"Find the vapour formed.","answer":"4,000 − 1,000 = 3,000 kg/h","explain":"Feed = product + vapour."},{"prompt":"Write the heat balance.","answer":"Steam heat + 4,000×40 = 3,000×640 + 1,000×100 → Steam heat = 19,20,000 + 1,00,000 − 1,60,000 = 18,60,000 kcal/h","explain":"Heat in = heat out."},{"prompt":"Find the steam required.","answer":"18,60,000 ÷ 540 = 3,444 kg/h","explain":"Steam = heat ÷ heat given per kg of steam."},{"prompt":"Find the steam economy.","answer":"3,000 ÷ 3,444 = 0.87","explain":"Economy = kg vapour ÷ kg steam."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L6 (modified)","order":1236},
{"id":"p1-b05-001","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What is the purpose of a material and energy balance?","back":"To assess input, conversion efficiency, output and losses; to quantify all material, energy and waste streams in a process or system; and to give a basis for improvement and savings.","explain":"An exam MCQ asks whether the objective is input-output, conversion efficiency or losses; the answer is 'all the above'. Energy balances let industry examine each stage of a process, the whole process, or the whole production chain from raw material to finished product.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p143","order":1237},
{"id":"p1-b05-002","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Trace how a material balance is used from the conceptual stage to production.","back":"It is estimated at the planning stage, improved during pilot-scale tests, verified during commissioning, and finally used as a control measure during production.","explain":"Material balances are fundamental to process control, especially control of product yields. The balance becomes more accurate as real plant data replaces design estimates.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p143","order":1238},
{"id":"p1-b05-003","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"In a material balance diagram of a process, on which side is the recycle stream shown?","back":"On the input side. A recycle stream is always treated as an input to the process.","explain":"In BEE's component diagram, the inputs are raw materials, chemicals, water/air, energy/power and recycled material. The outputs are products, by-products, gaseous emissions, wastewater, liquid waste for recycle, reusable waste for other operations, and solid waste for storage or disposal. A by-product is never an input.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p143","order":1239},
{"id":"p1-b05-004","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Write the general mass balance, and explain what 'losses' means when a balance does not close.","back":"Mass in = mass out + mass stored, i.e. raw materials = products + wastes + stored materials. If it doesn't close: raw materials = products + waste products + stored products + losses, where losses are unidentified materials.","explain":"The book's sugar plant example: if sugar entering is not matched by purified sugar plus sugar in waste liquors plus sugar accumulated, then sugar is either being chemically changed (burned) or going unnoticed down the drain. With no chemical change, the balance also holds for each individual component.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p144","order":1240},
{"id":"p1-b05-005","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"State the general energy balance for a unit operation.","back":"Energy in = energy out + energy stored. Energy out includes energy leaving with products, with wastes, and lost to the surroundings.","explain":"Energy balances are more complicated than mass balances because forms of energy can be inter-converted (e.g. mechanical energy to heat), but overall the quantities must balance.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p145","order":1241},
{"id":"p1-b05-006","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish steady-state from unsteady-state, and continuous from batch processes.","back":"Steady state: no process variable changes with time. Unsteady state: variables change with time. Continuous: feed and product streams move in and out all the time (e.g. oil refinery, distillation). Batch: feed is charged, processed with no mass added or removed, and products removed at a set time or condition.","explain":"In continuous processes, balances are written per unit time. Assuming steady state means flows and vessel hold-ups do not change with time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p146","order":1242},
{"id":"p1-b05-007","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"At what three levels can material balances be developed, and what decides which materials get priority?","back":"Overall (whole plant), section-wise (department or cost centre) and equipment-wise. Priority is decided mainly by material cost: costly materials before cheap ones, and products before waste materials.","explain":"Section-wise balances help prioritise focus areas for efficiency improvement. Equipment-wise balances help assess equipment performance and identify energy and material losses.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p146","order":1243},
{"id":"p1-b05-008","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What are the typical steps in a material balance procedure?","back":"a) Define the basis and units (mass for a batch, flow rate for continuous); b) draw a flowchart with a boundary so streams in and out can be identified; c) write the material balance equations.","explain":"First identify materials in, out and stored, then decide whether to balance the gross material or individual constituents. For example, a balance can be done for dry solids alone by splitting the material into water and non-water.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p147","order":1244},
{"id":"p1-b05-009","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"Define weight/weight concentration, weight/volume concentration, molar concentration and mole fraction.","back":"w/w: weight of solute ÷ total weight of solution. w/v: weight of solute in the total volume of solution. Molar concentration: number of molecular weights of solute (in kg) in 1 m³ of solution. Mole fraction: moles of solute ÷ total moles of all species.","explain":"For gases, concentrations are mainly given as weight per unit volume or as partial pressures. For dilute solutions, the mole fraction is close to moles of solute ÷ moles of solvent.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p147","order":1245},
{"id":"p1-b05-010","paper":1,"topic":"Material & energy balance","kind":"fact","front":"What should a process flowchart for a material and energy balance show?","back":"Process steps in sequence from raw material to finished product, intermediates and by-products, operating parameters (temperature, pressure, % concentration), flow rates of all streams (m³/h or kg/h), the total cycle time for batch processes, wastes, and the final output.","explain":"Identifying and drawing the unit operation or process is a prerequisite for any material and energy balance. Inputs include raw materials, water, steam and energy. An energy and mass balance diagram should be drawn for each step and for the whole plant.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 05 p148","order":1246}
]$cards$::jsonb);
